	<strings name="main_menu/settings_select/player_setup/player_profile_edit/video_settings/brightness/help_strings">
		<string text=""/>
		<string text=""/>
		<string text="How bright the picture is, the HUD and menus too, as Halo PC's gamma. Higher brightens the dark parts; lower darkens them. 1.0 shows the game as it is drawn. With HDR on an HDR display, Paper White sets the brightness instead.|Click the number (or press A) to type any from 0.5 to 2.0."/>
		<string text="10-Bit draws finer shades, so fog and skies show no bands: a 10-bit display shows them all, others a fine grain. 8-Bit draws as before, lighter on the graphics card. Auto picks for you: 10-Bit, or 8-Bit with battery saver.|HDR has its own depth: this applies with HDR off."/>
		<string text="Smoke, dust, steam and weather fade softly where they meet walls and floors, at a small cost. Off draws them as the Xbox did: a hard line where they cut in."/>
		<string text=""/>
		<string text="Brighter highlights on an HDR display. Auto turns it on while the display shows HDR; a display without HDR shows the game as it was."/>
		<string text="Faithful keeps the picture and brightens its highlights. Punchy lifts the whole scene, as a remaster would.|The pictures are approximate: SDR cannot show HDR."/>
		<string text="With HDR, how bright white surfaces and the HUD are, in nits. System matches your system's SDR brightness. Raise it in a bright room."/>
		<string text="With HDR, how bright the HUD and the menus are, in nits, apart from the picture. Paper White keeps them where paper white is. The HUD's glows stay as far above it."/>
		<string text="With HDR, how dark the darkest shadows are: the curve an SDR display shows them by. 2.2 is most PC monitors' and TVs', darker; sRGB is a Mac's. Auto picks sRGB on a Mac, 2.2 elsewhere."/>
		<string text="With HDR, the brightest your display shows, in nits, for a display that reports a wrong peak. System uses the one it reports. Only the highlights change."/>
		<string text="With HDR, Expanded lets the brightest colors, like plasma and needler shards, grow more vivid than sRGB, keeping their hue. sRGB keeps the picture as it is."/>
		<string text="With HDR, brighter lights of the game's own: the sun's flares, glows, glowing particles, vehicle lights and panels. Off keeps the picture as it is. The HUD and menus stay as they are."/>
		<string text=""/>
		<string text="The brightest the display shows, and its SDR white, as the system reports them."/>
	</strings>
</menus>
